<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich Bullinger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1504–1575</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinrich Bullinger was a Swiss Reformer who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> as head of the Zürich church in 1531 and led it until his death. Through a vast correspondence he advised Reformed churches across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consensus-tigurinus">Consensus Tigurinus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> in 1549, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-helvetic-confession">Second Helvetic Confession</a> of 1566 was adopted by Reformed churches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger.jpg" alt="Heinrich Bullinger" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Asper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Bullinger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
